  alt          s/r_M 1.96-8.00: N =    453  b_perp/b_par - 1 = -0.1081 +- 0.0728 (1.5 sigma, p = 0.172; bootstrap would say +- 0.0384 = 2.8 sigma)  PAR larger   [MG predicts PAR larger]

  THE ONE LARGE NUMBER IN THIS SCRIPT, AND WHY IT IS NOT A DETECTION.  The widest bin returns a parallel-dominant
  split of -0.1530, which is the MG arm's registered sign in the bin where it is registered.  Three things say
  it is not the framework's:
    (i)   on the correct null it is p = 0.090 (1.7 sigma), not the 3.9 sigma a bootstrap claims;
    (ii)  it is 39 times LARGER than the whole predicted effect, which no correct theory can be;
    (iii) it shrinks monotonically as the chance-alignment cut is tightened and as the velocity tail is trimmed,
          which is what a contamination artefact does and not what a gravitational anisotropy does.
    contamination ladder  R_chance_align < 0.010: N =  372   split = -0.1530
    contamination ladder  R_chance_align < 0.003: N =  238   split = -0.1156
    contamination ladder  R_chance_align < 0.001: N =  151   split = -0.1007
    tail trim             drop top  0 v~:            N =  372   split = -0.1530
    tail trim             drop top  3 v~:            N =  369   split = -0.1422
    tail trim             drop top 10 v~:            N =  362   split = -0.1137
    tail trim             drop top 25 v~:            N =  347   split = -0.0677
  [PASS] 15-B the radius-resolved sign flip is NOT measurable, and the apparent signal in the widest bin does not survive its own controls.  The MG arm predicts the widest pairs (s > 1.96 r_M) reverse to PARALLEL-dominance -- a sign change inside one dataset, which would be far more distinctive than the aggregate split because no systematic knows where a pair's MOND radius is.  What the data show there is parallel-dominance of the right SIGN and utterly the wrong SIZE, at p = 0.09 on the random-axis null, shrinking under both the contamination cut and the tail trim   (s > 1.96 r_M: N = 372, split = -0.1530 +- 0.0918 (1.7 sigma, p = 0.090); the predicted magnitude is at most 0.0040, so the measured number is 39x too large to be it; tightening R_chance_align to 0.001 moves it to -0.1007)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MUTATION CONTROLS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] M15-1 NEWTONIAN CONTROL: at 0.5-1 kAU the internal field is far above a_0 and BOTH theories predict exactly zero split.  The measured control split is consistent with zero, so the estimator is not manufacturing an anisotropy out of the survey geometry -- and its error bar there is the systematics floor for the deep bins   (0.5-1 kAU (median internal y = 142.8, i.e. 27x the deep sample's): b_perp/b_par - 1 = +0.0131 +- 0.0107 (1.2 sigma) on 10026 pairs)
  [PASS] M15-2 RANDOM-AXIS control -- and it caught a bug in this script's first version.  The bootstrap and the random-axis null AGREE in the deep sample, where there are thousands of pairs spread over the sky, and DISAGREE by a factor of a few in the sparse widest bin, where the two orientation classes are two patches of sky rather than two samples of one.  The first version used the bootstrap everywhere and reported the widest bin as a 3.9 sigma detection of the pre-registered sign; on the correct null it is under 2 sigma.  A vacuous estimator would have given zero scatter here, and it does not   (deep sample: random-axis spread 0.0105 vs bootstrap 0.0136 (ratio 0.78); widest bin: random-axis 0.0918 vs bootstrap 0.0396 (ratio 2.32) -- the bootstrap is too small by that factor exactly where it mattered)
  injection +0.0040 (the pre-registered MG maximum): recovered +0.0041 above the un-injected baseline (bias +2.6%), which is 0.39 sigma -- INVISIBLE at this sample size
  injection +0.0362 (the superseded MI value): recovered +0.0371 above the un-injected baseline (bias +2.5%), which is 3.53 sigma -- DETECTED at this sample size
  injection +0.1000 (a 10% anisotropy, far above anything predicted): recovered +0.1025 above the un-injected baseline (bias +2.5%), which is 9.74 sigma -- DETECTED at this sample size
  [PASS] M15-3 INJECTION -- the mutation that decides between 'null' and 'underpowered', and it says UNDERPOWERED.  A 10% anisotropy written into the same pairs is recovered by the estimator at high significance and with a few per cent bias, so the pipeline works; the pre-registered MG-arm anisotropy written into the same pairs is recovered at a fraction of a sigma, so the pipeline cannot see what the framework actually predicts.  Item 15 is not a null result about the framework -- it is a statement about Gaia DR3's sample size   (injected +0.100 -> recovered +0.1025 (9.7 sigma, bias +2.5%); injected +0.00397 -> recovered +0.00407 (0.39 sigma, i.e. 8x below the 3 sigma threshold))
  [PASS] M15-4 ECLIPTIC-POLE axis, a NAMED systematic rather than a random one: Gaia's scanning law, and hence its proper-motion error field, is organised in ecliptic coordinates, so an instrumental anisotropy would show up about this axis before any other.  It does not, at this precision -- which is a bound on the systematic, not a proof of its absence   (ecliptic-pole split -0.0115 +- 0.0105 (1.1 sigma) vs the Galactic-centre +0.0068 +- 0.0105)
    balance check  median 1/SNR             perp     0.087531   par     0.087099   ratio 1.0050
    balance check  median R_chance_align    perp   7.0426e-05   par   6.6848e-05   ratio 1.0535
    balance check  median distance [pc]     perp       178.88   par       176.52   ratio 1.0134
    balance check  median M_tot [Msun]      perp       1.2354   par       1.2331   ratio 1.0018
    balance check  median sep [AU]          perp       3793.1   par       3703.8   ratio 1.0241
  [PASS] 15-balance the two orientation classes are matched in everything the estimator is sensitive to -- noise level, chance-alignment probability, distance, mass and separation -- so the split above is a measurement of orientation and not of a covariate that happens to correlate with it   (largest fractional imbalance among noise level and separation = 2.41%)

====================================================================================================================
ITEM 15 -- verdict
====================================================================================================================
  The list scored item 15 against the MODIFIED-INERTIA eigenvalues (gamma 1.12 vs 1.21, split 0.042-0.047 boost
  units).  On the operative MODIFIED-GRAVITY arm the pre-registered sample-level split is +0.0013 to +0.0046 --
  ten to thirty-six times smaller -- and DR3 measures the split to +-0.0122.  The item is therefore
  NOT RUNNABLE at DR3, by two to three orders of magnitude in sample size, and the same arithmetic says DR4
  will not close it either.  What DOES survive is the radius-resolved prediction of Part B: a SIGN FLIP inside
  one dataset at s = 1.96 r_M, which no instrumental systematic can imitate because no systematic knows where a
  pair's MOND radius is.  That is the version worth carrying forward, and it needs the same larger sample.
  [PASS] 15 VERDICT -- NOT RUNNABLE / UNDERPOWERED, recorded as such and not as a null.  The measurement is clean, the controls all pass, the injection test proves the estimator works at 10% and is blind at 0.4%, and the honest statement is that Gaia DR3 cannot address the pre-registered orientation falsifier on the arm that is actually in force.  The hunt list's own scoring criterion for item 15 rests on a superseded arm and should be retired   (measured +0.0079 +- 0.0122 boost units vs a predicted +0.0013 to +0.0046; 3 sigma needs 63x-793x this sample)

